**Read:** [A Heat Transfer Textbook — Lienhard and Lienhard](../sources/A-Heat-Transfer-Textbook-Lienhard.lingua#r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD), the entire book and sustained independent exercises; [Intermediate Heat and Mass Transfer — MIT 2.51](../sources/Intermediate-Heat-and-Mass-Transfer-MIT-2-51.lingua#r_4NKJ30PDFTY42GJVZX1C2ZS4B8), the full reading/calendar progression; [Second Level Course in Heat Transfer — R. P. Vedula](../sources/Second-Level-Heat-Transfer-Vedula.lingua#r_663BY2CF56BBB35C1S595A2VRF), the complete analytical course.

Choose a geometry that requires more than one heat-transfer mechanism. Begin with local/integral energy conservation, constitutive laws and complete initial/boundary/interface conditions. Explain every simplification through a scale, symmetry or independent observation.

Recover a conduction, convection or radiation limit that can be checked analytically. Resolve the same model numerically with refinement, energy closure and independent comparison. Then alter a physical condition that distinguishes competing models instead of merely comparing two implementations of the same approximation.

Account for sensor response, uncertain properties and contact or flow conditions before claiming agreement with measurement. State whether the work verifies mathematics, supports a physical model or demonstrates an actual device. Correct a discrepancy and document how the correction changes the predicted service, then continue to the advanced studies.

